  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1110 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nThe dry pattern finally starts to change on Friday as a boundary \nmoves into the area and along with a moisture axis. While the \nsurface boundary moves through the area on Friday, the boundary \naloft will linger through the weekend with multiple disturbances \nalong the quasi zonal upper flow. This will lead to rounds of \nshowers and isolated storms through Sunday when a deeper wave \npushes the boundary far enough south to put an end to our rain \nchances. Ensembles have the highest rainfall amounts along the \nplateau with lower amounts in the west and northwest. Locations \nalong the plateau have about a 50-65% chance of picking up at \nleast one inch of rain through the weekend. Locations in the west \nand northwest have about a 30-40% chance on picking up an inch of\nrain. With the better dynamics remaining well north of the area \nand poor lapse rates due to saturated soundings, severe storms are\nnot anticipated.\n\nBehind the weekend system, temperatures will be back closer to \nnormal with highs in the 70s and lows in the 40s and 50s. The area \nwill be dry for the remainder of the forecast.|",
